                "event": "Flash Flood Warning",
                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Charleston WV",
                "headline": "Flash Flood Warning issued September 10 at 8:01AM EDT until September 10 at 11:00AM EDT by NWS Charleston WV",
                "description": "FFWRLX\n\nThe National Weather Service in Charleston has extended the\n\n* Flash Flood Warning for...\nCentral Gallia County in southeastern Ohio...\nSouthern Meigs County in southeastern Ohio...\nWest Central Jackson County in northwestern West Virginia...\nNorthern Mason County in western West Virginia...\n\n* Until 1100 AM EDT.\n\n* At 801 AM EDT, emergency management reported multiple water rescues\nin Jackson County due to thunderstorms that produced heavy rain\nacross the warned area earlier this morning. Between 4 and 7 inches\nof rain have fallen. Flooding impacts will continue, with light\nrainfall continuing to be possible. Flash flooding is already\noccurring.\n\nHAZARD...Flash flooding caused by thunderstorms.\n\nSOURCE...Emergency management reported.\n\nIMPACT...Flash flooding of small creeks and streams, urban areas,\nhighways, streets and underpasses as well as other poor\ndrainage and low-lying areas.\n\n* Some locations that will experience flash flooding include...\nPoint Pleasant, Gallipolis, Rio Grande, New Haven, Middleport,\nHenderson, Leon, Cheshire, Adamsville, Lakin, Gallopolis Ferry,\nKyger, Letart, Southside, Mount Alto, Gage, and Centerpoint.",
                "instruction": "Turn around, don't drown when encountering flooded roads. Most flood\ndeaths occur in vehicles.\n\nStay away or be swept away. River banks and culverts can become\nunstable and unsafe.\n\nReport flooding to the National Weather Service by calling toll free,\n800 401 9535, when you can do so safely.",
                "response": "Avoid",
                "note": null,
